      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The Stoller peripheral neurostimulation (SANS) is a new therapeutic procedure for bladder dysfunction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Each of 11 patients (8 women, 3 men) underwent 12 SANS treatment sessions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INDICATIONS",
          "content": "overactive bladder (5 patients), chronic nonobstructive urinary retention (3 patients) and pelvic pain (3 patients). Median follow-up was 3 months."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In 2 overactive bladder patients, the number of voids was reduced by at least 50%. In one patient with chronic retention, residual urine was temporarily decreased to <100 cc. 2 pelvic pain patients reported a slight improvement. In summary, 50% of the patients demonstrated a temporary response. Only 2 overactive bladder patients reported a permanent objective and subjective improvement. No complications were observed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "As the success rate was low and the procedure is time-consuming, we recommend the SANS procedure only in selected cases. We perform SANS treatment only in patients with overactive bladder refractory to conservative treatment. Prerequisites for a successful treatment is a high patient motivation."
        }
      ]},
